Bitcoin Trend Detection Trading System - Project Notes

Started with a boring school assignment - BYOP. Everyone's doing student performance predictors.

Then realized - I have 7.5M rows of Bitcoin 1-minute OHLC data. That's actually useful.

Initial thought: can I predict next minute direction? Scalp like I do in options.
Nope. 1-minute crypto data is too noisy. Spreads and fees would kill any micro edge.

ChatGPT was right - scalping won't work here. Switched to trend detection instead.

Real question became: "is a real trend starting right now?" Not "will price go up next tick".

Thought about labels. Labels are everything. Model only learns what labels teach it.

Started simple: just label price goes up or down. Too noisy.

Better idea: define profitability. Encode risk management INTO the labels themselves.

Triple barrier set up. TP and SL thresholds. Look 30 minutes ahead.

But didn't want symmetric thresholds. System needs to be cautious.
TP = +1.5% (hard to trigger BUY, means strong move exists)
SL = -0.7% (easy to trigger SELL, detects danger early)

Why? Avoiding bad trades > chasing every possible trade. Simple as that.

Naturally creates imbalance: more SELL than BUY. That's intentional. Bias toward caution.

Model output design: initially thought softmax. But softmax forces a choice even when signals weak.

Sigmoid is better. Two independent outputs. Can say "both weak, don't trade". That's reality.

Label logic ended up clean:
- Look 30 minutes ahead
- Price hits +1.5% first? = BUY
- Price hits -0.7% first? = SELL
- Neither? = do nothing (no trade)

ChatGPT kept saying: don't expect 90% accuracy. Markets random. 50-65% is actually strong.

Real edge isn't from accuracy. Real edge is feature quality + uncertainty filtering. Trade less, trade better.

Pipeline became:
1. Get labels
2. Engineer features (what patterns show trend starting?)
3. Train model for probabilities
4. Apply thresholds for decisions
5. Backtest for actual profits
6. Then we know if it's real

Must remember:
- No future data in features (data leakage trap)
- Time-based splits, never shuffle
- Class weights for training (imbalance is intentional)
- Test small before full scale
- Uncertainty filter matters as much as predictions

implemented full labelling code. Realized later having a third signal where the model just refuses to trade would also be good.
Not hittign either stop loss or take profit within max horizon would trigger refusal signal marked as "idk_label" for weak trend and low deviation signal

Ran programme labelling.py and noticed: overalapping between the three signals is 0 so code logic is sound, no trade ratio is 0.9322 or 93.22%. meaning the resultimg machine learning model will play it very cautious, just as we intended.

implemented every single feature I could think of in financial metrics and indicators
relevence score is quite low for many of he features but for XGBoost those should still help fine tune and improve the accuracy, plus, relavence is a linear thing. we don't know how usefull these features might be wehn used in correlation to the other features. the relevance scores that XGBoost will produce for us might be a much better indicator of which features are actually irrelavent or insignificant.
For now I don't feel the need to scale back on the features. but if we move to deep learnign in the future then features will be cut back and normalized.
I will have to be careful while normalizing to avoid data leakage.

Implemented XGBoost to predict probability of buy and probability of sell using two seperate models
we did not train for idk_label becasue it is derived form buy and sell signal so it is redundant

Timestamp had accidentally made its way into the training data becasue of a typo. Fixed it and will train again.

Trained on full 200k row test set. Results came back and initial reaction was concerning.

BUY model: ROC-AUC 0.6470 which is decent but precision is terrible at 0.14. Means 86% false positives. Also recall is 0.10 so only catches 10% of real buys. The uncertainty filter (> 0.7) is so strict that zero buy trades were generated.

SELL model: ROC-AUC 0.5779 which is basically random. Precision 0.29 (71% false positives) and recall 0.87 (catches most real sells but floods with noise). Generated 19026 trades out of 40k samples which is 47%. Way too many.

Feature importance dominated by ATR (0.28-0.45). Everything else is noise. Bollinger bands marginally help but rest of 30+ indicators seem useless.

Initial thought: model is broken, need to tweak. But then realized something important.

In BUY model: only 2461 positive samples out of 40k. That's like training a classifier with almost no examples of the thing we want to predict. And SELL model had even worse class imbalance.

With full 7.5M rows: BUY samples would be ~75k (30x more). SELL samples would be 300k+ (25x more).

This might not be a model problem at all. Could just be a data quantity problem. 200k rows is not enough to learn patterns when positive cases are so rare.

Decided to test on full 7.5M rows on friend's gaming laptop. Should give us 30x more training data for both BUY and SELL signals. If ROC-AUC improves significantly (65%+), then we know the issue was data quantity, not the approach. This is worth testing before tweaking parameters or changing the whole labeling strategy.

---

**FULL DATASET RESULTS - PARAMETER TUNING PHASE**

Got the results back from 7.5M full training:
- BUY ROC-AUC: 0.8480 (was 0.6470 on 200k subset) → +30.9% improvement ✓
- SELL ROC-AUC: 0.8149 (was 0.5779 on 200k subset) → +40.8% improvement ✓

Data quantity hypothesis confirmed.

Key insight: Even with better ROC-AUC, model is heavily biased toward "no trade". BUY labels only 0.54% of data, SELL only 4.76%. The uncertainty filter (0.7/0.3 thresholds) kills trade signals - zero BUY trades generated because model never reaches 70% confidence on such rare samples.

**PARAMETER TUNING STRATEGY**

Instead of widening TP/SL gap (which creates MORE idk labels), we're going asymmetric and expanding horizon:
- Increase MAX_HORIZON: 30 → 60 minutes (more time for trends to form)
- Tighten SL: -0.7% → -0.5% (protective signal triggers earlier)
- Reduce TP: +1.5% → +0.8% (lower bar for BUY confirmation)

This generates more labels without creating market chop situations. Updated labelling.py with these parameters. Next: Re-run labeling → features → model training.

---

**PARAMETER TUNING RESULTS (200k subset)**

Applied new thresholds: TP=0.8%, SL=0.5%, MAX_HORIZON=60min

**Label Distribution Improvement:**
- BUY ratio: 8.27% (was ~0.54% before) → 15x more BUY labels ✓
- SELL ratio: 20.01% (was ~4.76% before) → 4x more SELL labels ✓

**Model Performance:**
- BUY ROC-AUC: 0.6728 (was 0.6470) → +4.1% improvement
- SELL ROC-AUC: 0.6278 (was 0.5779) → +8.6% improvement

**Trade Generation (with uncertainty filter):**
- First run: 8 BUY + 16,127 SELL = 16,135 trades (40.35%)
- Second run: 94 BUY + 31,594 SELL = 31,688 trades (79.24%)

**Issues:**
- BUY model still weak: precision 0.25, recall 0.13 (catches few real buys)
- SELL model problematic: precision 0.28, recall 0.93 (floods with false positives)
- 79% trade rate is way too high for a cautious system
- Feature importance still ATR-dominated (40-54%)

**Decision Point:** Is this satisfactory for full dataset training, or need further tuning?

**DECISION: PROCEED WITH FULL DATASET TRAINING**

Current 200k results are promising enough to believe full 7.5M training will produce a good model. The label distribution improved dramatically (15x more BUY, 4x more SELL signals), and we know from previous testing that scaling to full data improves ROC-AUC by 30-40%. With better label ratios now, expect even stronger results.

**Expected Full Dataset Performance:**
- BUY ratio: ~8% (600k samples) vs current 8.27%
- SELL ratio: ~20% (1.5M samples) vs current 20.01%
- ROC-AUC improvement: 30-40% like before
- Trade frequency: Should be more reasonable with better model confidence

Training on full dataset now...

